return [
    'packages_diagnostics_heading' => 'Installation diagnostics',
    'packages_diagnostics_current' => 'For troubleshooting on the client, use the output logs of executed child scripts and their own logs. The package template writes output logs under %ProgramData%\\VirtuSphere\\Logs for system installations and %LOCALAPPDATA%\\VirtuSphere\\Logs for user installations. AppEnforce.log records the MECM installation invocation; AppDiscovery.log records detection. A successful invocation does not confirm later work performed by a scheduled task.',
    'packages_portal_reports' => 'The portal diagnostics are in place: the "Package reports" column of the catalog leads to the reported attempts per package and version. The reports come from the client; the portal does not read files from the client. Until the package reporter is set up on the clients, the reports stay empty. A missing report proves neither an installation failure nor a connection failure.',
    'link_reports' => 'Go to package reports',
    'packages_heading' => 'Packages & versions',
    'packages_p1' => 'Packages are not created in the portal but on the MECM server. There the autoimporter reads every package folder with its config.json (required fields ProjectName and version; the package template also requires ErrorAction set to Stop or Continue, or the installation on the VM aborts) and builds an MECM application. A device collection with its deployment is created only with generateOwnDeviceColletion; without it the package is missing from the portal catalog, because the package sync reads collections only. Its name is composed from both fields: ProjectName, a hyphen, then the version, for example "Firefox-115.0". The package sync reports exactly these collection names via HTTP to the portal, which only displays the catalog and links packages to VMs. The exact flow of the autoimporter and the package sync is described only in the technical project documentation; the portal contains no flowcharts for it.',
    'packages_p1b' => 'The portal reverses this composition to show base name and version separately and to keep multiple versions of the same package distinguishable. It splits at the last hyphen: everything before it is the base name, everything after it is the version. This is necessary because the base name itself may contain hyphens (such as "Adobe-Reader-DC") while the version may not. Only under this rule is the last hyphen unambiguously the boundary between name and version. For package maintenance on the MECM server this means: in config.json the version must not contain a hyphen, while the ProjectName may.',
    'packages_p2' => 'If a package disappears from MECM it is not deleted but marked as "Retired"; existing VM assignments are kept. VirtuSphere never moves a VM onto a new version on its own: the new version would run its install.ps1 again on a configured system and overwrite what was set up by hand afterwards. A human therefore always decides in the VM editor which version a VM gets. Template VMs, which are never installed, are the exception: they follow a higher version exactly once, when it appears in the catalog for the first time. If a template is later set back to an older version by hand, it stays there.',
    'packages_p2b' => 'Retired packages are removed for good after :days days, but only those currently assigned to no VM whose assignments were never relinked. A row that was assigned before and whose assignment someone removed by hand is therefore removed as well. A row whose assignments were once moved to a successor automatically (today only for templates, earlier also for VMs) is kept permanently; otherwise the history would become untraceable once the cleanup window passed.',
    'packages_p3' => 'The package list can be filtered by status (Active/Retired). The VM editor hides retired packages. If a VM is still linked to a retired version it stays visible, and a warning explains that a transfer to MECM with this box ticked stays stuck in the queue. If a higher version of the same package is active, a hint names it, even while the linked version is still active. The log records every retirement of a version that VMs still hold, with the number of VMs.',
    'packages_os_heading' => 'Operating systems',
    'packages_os_p1' => 'Like packages, operating systems are not maintained in the portal; they come from MECM. The same package sync also reports every Task Sequence on the MECM server, and from these the operating system catalog is built, which the "Operating Systems" page displays. An entry is only effective when its name matches a Task Sequence exactly, because the operating system assigned to a VM selects that Task Sequence for the PXE boot. That is why entries cannot be created or edited here: a hand-typed name without a matching Task Sequence would point nowhere at PXE boot.',
    'packages_os_p2' => 'New Task Sequences appear automatically on the next sync (cadence set by the PackagesSyncIntervalSeconds parameter of the MECM installation, plus a full reconciliation every hour). When a Task Sequence is deleted in MECM, the entry does not disappear but becomes "Retired"; assigned VMs keep their choice, and the entry is only removed after :days days once no VM still uses it. An administrator can also delete an entry by hand: if the Task Sequence still exists in MECM, the next sync re-creates it (self-healing); if the sync is down (MECM outage), it stays missing until it returns, without harming existing VMs, whose assignment uses the stored name. If all Task Sequences are removed or MECM currently returns none, the catalog stays unchanged instead of being emptied.',
